    "body": "## Summary\n- add a real HTTP + PostgreSQL crash/retry smoke for `JOB_PUBLICATION` Stripe settlement\n- reproduce and fix a real flush-ordering bug discovered by that smoke: `payment_received_at` was set while the publication still had `PAYMENT_REQUIRED`, then a nested wallet `saveAndFlush()` exposed that transient state to PostgreSQL and violated `chk_job_publications_recovery_state`\n- keep `PAYMENT_REQUIRED + payment_received_at=NULL` through flush-capable reservation/job/escrow writes, then move atomically to `PAYMENT_RECEIVED` or `PUBLISHED` and record the successful payment timestamp\n- add unit regression tests that assert the publication is still in the database-valid unpaid state at `restoreReservation()` and `createJob()` boundaries\n- create a partially wallet-funded publication, deliver a correctly signed synthetic `payment_intent.succeeded` event through the real webhook endpoint, and force a later PostgreSQL failure at final escrow creation\n- prove the failed webhook returns HTTP 500 and every write from that settlement rolls back while the original publication/reservation remains intact\n- remove the failpoint and retry the exact same signed Stripe event, proving exactly one payment claim, job, HELD escrow and funding/ledger chain is committed\n- deliver the same event a third time and prove idempotent `already processed` behavior with no duplicate money movement\n\n## Production bug fixed\nBefore this PR, a partially wallet-funded publication could fail settlement even without the intentional crash failpoint. `recordSuccessfulPayment()` ran before reservation restoration. `WalletService` subsequently executed `saveAndFlush()`, causing Hibernate to flush the managed publication in an intermediate state that the V43 database invariant deliberately rejects: `status=PAYMENT_REQUIRED` with a non-null `payment_received_at`.\n\nThe fix changes ordering rather than weakening the database constraint. Flush-capable dependent writes run while the publication remains a valid `PAYMENT_REQUIRED` row. Only after those writes succeed does the publication transition to its paid terminal/recovery state. The whole operation remains one transaction, so later failure still rolls everything back.\n\n## Schema\nNo migration. The existing V43 constraint is correct and is retained unchanged.\n\n## Merge gate\nMerge only after the publication payment smoke including the forced rollback/retry scenario, API tests, CodeQL and full runtime checks are green on the exact PR head, master is unchanged, branch is 0 behind, and merge uses the exact expected head SHA.",
